WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
Crystalis, the time has come for you to fulfill your destiny.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:29.040
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.040
I'm Mike.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.380
I'm Sean. And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:38.000
And the game, as I mentioned at the top of the episode, like we always do, Crystallis.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.520
Crystalis, right? Crystalis, yeah.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.320
Crystalis? Crystalis.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.660
It definitely is a...

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.820
Do we know what a Crystalis is?

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.160
I think it's like a sword.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.960
Yes. It's a combination of four elemental swords.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.200
Or like a KFC in Taco Bell.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.620
Crystalis. And thankfully it's not activated by calling its name or else some people might be confused

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.140
because this is like a...

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:12.040
It's funny because Faxanadu, which some people still say Faxanadu, or whatever.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.560
What did you say, Joe? You said Faxanadu?

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.360
Faxanadu, yeah. I said Faxanadu.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.960
Sure. I think everybody in the world other than Mike has said Faxanadu.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.120
Well, I just know that it comes from a series of games called Xanadu.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.520
Yeah. And then Samacon Xanadu.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.160
Right, right, right. But anyway, I digress.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:35.140
The point being that Faxanadu was a like Zelda 2 style clone that like we had heard rumblings

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.380
about being a good game before we played it and then we confirmed it was a good game

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:40.720
and put it on the essential games list.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.320
Crystalis is another one where like I heard good things about it going in and it's not

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.180
a Zelda 2 thing, but it is definitely like a Zelda...

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:57.140
I wouldn't even say Zelda 1, but like just later Legend of Zelda games inspired like type

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.120
game.

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:04.480
And so, you know, there's some kind of weird combination here between the titling of your

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.600
game and, you know, making it subjective how one might pronounce it and if it's a Zelda

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.840
clone... Being tied to Zelda? Yeah.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:21.160
I'll say that I think that it does have maybe as much DNA from Zelda 2 as Zelda 1.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.240
Okay. There's a lot of things that Zelda 2 and I haven't played many of the or any of the later

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:30.060
Zelda games, but just from one I'm familiar with, correct me if I'm wrong, there are a

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.920
lot of things that Zelda 2 started in the Zelda lore.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.620
Like, I mean, besides, you know, obviously there's side scrolling in Zelda 2, but also

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:42.820
there's like the towns and there's like more of a straight, a linear story.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.060
Yes. And this kind of felt more like that, but like with a control style of Zelda 1.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:55.220
Sure, but in this one, you go into the house of an old man and he gives you a sword, which

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.020
sounds awfully familiar to Zelda 1, right?

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.020
Well, yeah. There's elements of both.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:01.680
No, I know. I'm teasing.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:08.920
I'm saying like that, if there were ever rules for what contains to be a Zelda clone,

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:13.340
getting a sword from an old man has to be on like the Ten Commandments.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.780
True. Sean, what do you make of all this?

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.260
Did you feel the Zelda-ness of it all?

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:25.120
Yeah, I mean, I was thinking more along the lines of like, oh, this is like Zelda 1 until

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.800
you said not so much that, but more the later ones.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:34.460
And now I see that, like more a link to the past.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.580
Whereas, yeah, I was just thinking like, oh, we've already played Zelda.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:40.960
I guess this feels pretty Zelda.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.180
I didn't really get anything from 2.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:50.040
I mean, I guess you could say that going to towns and speaking to people, but that's more

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.420
just like a RPG trope in general.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.700
So I guess I didn't make that connection.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:02.000
But yeah, it definitely like bears its inspirations on its sleeve.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.180
And for some reason, and I don't know how to articulate this too well, but it feels to

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:16.340
me like even though I know this is an NES game, played it on an NES, have played many NES games,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:24.440
something about it felt very much like the Legend of Zelda Link's Awakening Game Boy game.

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:30.840
And I don't know if it's the graphical style of it or the size of the characters, but I had

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:38.200
a very like Game Boy color feel to this game that I don't usually feel with NES games.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:47.200
And I know the Game Boy is basically just like a powered down version of the NES and was working

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:53.460
off of that. But this game more than other games has given me that vibe of like, you throw this on a

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.600
portable console, portable handheld, and I'm right there.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:58.640
It's funny you say that.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.580
Oh God. Because we both said it's funny you say that.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:06.440
I feel like we might be about, should we try and say the rest of the sentence at the

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.760
same time? No, this is all you, Joe.

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:12.680
It gave me Super Nintendo vibes going almost the opposite direction.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:23.440
Yeah, I'll say that. Okay, so I think that the level of graphical polish on this game, like you've got particle

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.500
effects everywhere.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:26.800
Uh, that kind of stuff.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:30.280
Like that was giving me Super NES vibes as Joe was saying.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:34.700
But what I thought you were going to mention, and I guess this will be talked about more

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:42.220
later in the episode when we talk about spinoffs and yada, uh, is that there, there was a Game

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:43.640
Boy color version of this game.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.380
I've done a bit more research than I normally do for this one.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.080
Uh, and it was vastly inferior.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:53.260
Um, so that's sort of where I thought you were going with it.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:56.100
And that is true, but we will get there.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.380
Maybe this is like the ideal version of that handheld version.

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:01.200
Yeah, yeah, this is, I don't know.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.060
There's something about it.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:07.400
Like it just mimics maybe the style of Link's Awakening to a little bit that I just felt

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:11.740
it. But, um, you know, I, I love Link's Awakening, so I'm, I'm here for it.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:16.780
And that's where, uh, what I wanted to get into next, I guess, is like, you know, an NES

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:19.320
game at any point.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:20.680
Now we played so many of them.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.200
Uh, you need a promising start, right?

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:24.300
Like we played a lot.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.400
So you need something that's going to stand out.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.820
And, uh, Crystalis was, was a game that didn't necessarily, you know, we talked about

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:36.600
it being a Zelda clone and everything didn't necessarily like break any new molds or anything,

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:46.760
but it plays well from the beginning and doesn't, doesn't really like, uh, slog or, uh, or force

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:52.800
you to do, uh, to do something that you, you know, that like progresses the gameplay in a

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:53.900
manner that you don't want to.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:59.060
I feel like you go right into the first town, you get what you need, you go out to adventure

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:04.740
and the rest is just kind of what we expect from, from video games now.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:06.780
Like, you know, you just, you just, you're in the game.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:07.820
Like you're just doing it.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:14.880
And it's not, uh, it's not, you know, it's not, um, not held back by its previous, uh,

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:16.820
by, you know, the age of the game.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:18.480
It felt like a game I was playing today.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:23.680
Yeah. Well, yeah. I mean, what I was expecting out of a game like this, and I know it's not a turn-based

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:30.160
game, so it was never going to be that, but there's enough standard JRPG DNA in this

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.180
game to make me think that like, oh God, it's going to make me open a menu to talk to

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:39.380
somebody or it's going to, oh God, it's going to make me open a menu and select search when

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:40.820
I'm looking for something on the ground.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:46.540
Like I thought that that was sort of what we were in for, but it's very streamlined in,

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.720
in how it's designed.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:55.800
Uh, I won't say that it, it's completely free of, of headaches because as we'll get

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:00.720
into it, I think there's a little too much, uh, focus on the grind.

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:08.260
But if we're just talking about how you interact with the game and like the, the, the level of

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:14.080
friction between you doing what you want to do and it happening on screen, I will agree

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.400
that, yeah, it, you just start doing it as Mike said.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:24.560
Yeah. It, to me, it felt the most modern in the sense that you can, yeah, what you're doing, you're

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.460
figuring it out because it's designed to have you figure it out.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:33.840
It's one of the first games like this where I did, I felt I did not need a guide.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:39.360
Um, even games that I've loved, uh, I mean, even Zelda 2, which I, which I obviously loved.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:42.280
I needed a guide. I think Faxanadu, I needed a guide.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.540
I can't remember. I think there was one other one that I played where I didn't feel like I needed a guide.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:51.040
I might've been Willow, but, uh, but this one, I did eventually start using a guide for

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.520
the sake of time because I wanted to get further before this record.

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.900
But I, I was, you know, even when I was lost, I was, it felt like a modern game where I could

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:01.360
kind of explore and figure out where to go next from context clues.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:06.240
And as I'm exploring, it doesn't feel like there's this hopeless, like, well, first of

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:12.720
all, when I'm exploring, I'm not just in like absolute moral, mortal peril every time where

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.000
a lot of NES games, it's like, you can't even afford to explore because it's so easy to die.

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:18.400
Or moral peril.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:19.920
Yeah. Or moral peril.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:26.380
But, uh, I, yeah, I felt like that coupled with the smoothness of the gameplay, which I, I, this

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:31.720
might be a hot take here, but I do think that they, as far as like being a, um, an overhead

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:39.500
game where you stab and fire your sword energy at people, uh, I think that they perfected that

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:42.920
original Zelda, uh, game feel.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:46.480
So like, those are the two things that made me feel like this was a little bit ahead of

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:52.460
its time. What's interesting about, um, you know, Sean was mentioning the grind and everything.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:59.220
And, uh, Joe, you were mentioning the combat is if you take in what Zelda one did in 87,

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.280
right, we're now three years away from that.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:09.260
And so it would be nice for a game to just even, you know, be as tight and, uh, and specially

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:17.000
designed as Zelda one felt like, but what Christalis does is it, it kind of works upon

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:20.280
three years, uh, of, you know, okay.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:23.500
So Zelda one has come and gone and now how do we improve upon that?

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:25.380
And so it took some chances on things.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:27.500
And I think it did change up a little bit.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:33.620
Like it doesn't like the combat doesn't feel exactly like, uh, Zelda one, but in a way,

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:38.100
even though it's, it's the similar, you know, you stab with the sword and you walk around,

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:42.960
uh, you know, because of the addition of your force meter and your magic and changing up

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:47.980
swords on elements and solving puzzles in ways that, you know, you could never have done in

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:54.760
the original Zelda. I do think that this offers an expansion in every way possible from that game, while also

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:58.520
including a full story, something Zelda did not have.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:03.140
And so you also have the ability, like you said, you don't need a guide because you get

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:09.940
your kind of clues on what to do next if necessary by talking to the people in the town or, you

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:14.640
know, it's given to you after you defeat a boss or something, uh, or receive an item.

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:17.000
You kind of like get that nudge of what to do next.

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:22.860
And so I do feel like even if this game is a clone, right?

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:25.780
And we could talk about the combat more in depth too.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:32.740
It doesn't necessarily have to, uh, go too far off from feeling, from feeling different

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:37.700
than Zelda because of just the way that like games have progressed since Zelda's release.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:39.880
And it, it takes, it works on other things too.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:45.320
Like it worked upon other versions of games that have tried to do the Zelda thing.

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:50.940
And here with Crystalis, like we're actually seeing that all together in a more complete picture.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:12:00.500
Yeah. I think that, but your use of the word clone, I think that that that's valid, but I also

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:07.160
kind of want to push back on it where I think that this, this game does it well enough where

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:13.660
I think we can just start calling this genre tropes instead of like, oh, this is a Zelda clone

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:20.880
where I think it kind of belittles what this game does, uh, to completely view it as a

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:24.040
in that lens of like, oh, just trying to do what Zelda did.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:33.240
Um, whereas I think like what Zelda did is now just like, kind of like a more of a, a genre

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:34.080
codifier.

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:42.120
Cause I, I think that this game is, is good enough to not need to be, uh, qualified like

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:43.460
that. You know what I mean? Yeah.

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:46.620
It definitely stands out as a totally different experience from Zelda.

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:50.840
Like you should, if you like, if you love this game, you should still play Zelda.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:53.500
You know, like, like it's not, you're right.

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:54.920
It's not trying to be the same thing.

00:12:55.080 --> 00:13:00.860
It's just using like sort of the same base gameplay style as far as like overhead stab

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:05.100
with a sword action RPG, I guess, action, whatever adventure game.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:11.920
Yeah. And one thing we haven't seen before, at least I can't remember a game, uh, doing this is,

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:17.180
uh, and maybe this is what you're mentioning with the grind earlier, Sean, when you are under

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:25.580
leveled for an area, it's not that it takes, uh, way more hits to defeat an enemy or, oh,

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.980
I just don't have the correct item to defeat this enemy.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:35.200
You may have the right sword, uh, for the job and you may have, uh, you know, 300 hits

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:37.940
on the guy. You're just not doing damage.

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:40.440
And it's something you've learned.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:44.220
I feel like kind of early on, if you try to move a little too fast without leveling up,

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:50.920
but that is the one thing that I questioned about, uh, you know, for progress in this game

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:58.000
is like, if the player can get to the area, why say that like the damage is completely null?

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:01.720
Like why not make it so that it's like, you know, oh yeah, you, you know, like you're in

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:06.620
this new area that's really tough and, uh, it may take 20 hits to defeat an enemy.

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:09.820
And so that's kind of like a sign that you should go backwards.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:13.220
But instead they just say, no, it doesn't do any damage period.

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:16.680
It's weird that it lets you go and do those things.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:21.180
But also there are certain points that unless you've hit a certain trigger,

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:27.220
either in the story or just like what's in your inventory, it will just say like,

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:28.700
Hey, you're not strong enough to go here.

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:34.460
And it's just weird that like, you let me go here and you're, you told me that

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:37.740
I can try to attack things and it just won't do any damage.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:39.740
But now you're just telling me I can't go there at all.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:50.280
It's a, it's a strange, uh, it's just not very, uh, it's not following its own rules in certain times.

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:55.680
Yeah. Because there are plenty of times where the game wants to stop you from going somewhere

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:59.820
and it puts an ice wall in front of you and you need a fire power up to get through it.

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:02.880
Or it puts something there where there's a contextual thing you need to get past it.

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:03.860
And that's fine.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:07.080
But there's like nothing, then they're just like, well, we didn't put anything here to block you.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.660
So we're just gonna, there's just gonna be a text box that says don't go here yet.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:11.460
It's not time yet. Who's saying that?

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:20.260
Yeah. Uh, and also, yeah, as far as like the, the level things, I would like for them to always be able to, you know, be like,

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:21.860
yeah, you're clearly not supposed to be here yet.

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:27.120
But like, if you really want to challenge yourself, they probably weren't thinking like this back then, but it is weird.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:32.600
It is weird to make things just literally invincible until you get to a certain level.

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:07.520
But outside of the grind to get to the appropriate level to keep moving on,

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:14.580
you do have to give them credit for how powered up you can feel as you continue to move through the game.

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:20.400
I mean, playing the game at the beginning with just the sword and eventually getting your first magic spell

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:30.140
and then working upon that, it does feel good to level up in a way that nowadays we just take for granted.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:34.540
With skill trees, you're always leveled up, got to get a new ability,

00:16:34.540 --> 00:16:38.460
got to have a new combo on my controller, right?

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:40.780
It's like, that didn't really exist then.

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:48.660
So it is nice that at the very least you feel like you're more powerful as you continue to level in the game.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:56.820
It kind of blew my mind when, like, because one aspect of the game is you can charge up your attack

00:16:56.820 --> 00:17:02.560
and you start off with this like, oh, if you charge it up fully, you can do a projectile just like Zelda.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:08.600
But what kind of blew my mind was when you finally get to a certain point

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:15.000
and then you get another little special move that, like, if you charge up twice,

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.840
you get something that's a bit more powerful.

00:17:18.100 --> 00:17:21.880
And, like, it's very simple, but something like that, like,

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:29.400
adds so much more depth to an actual progression system that we don't really see a lot of

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:32.880
outside of just level one, two, three, yada, yada.

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:36.340
So that was just cool to see that.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:40.880
Yeah. And those, like, all correspond to different weapons with different elements.

00:17:41.020 --> 00:17:43.440
So not only are they getting more powerful, you also have to think, like,

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:45.080
okay, what's weak against this sword?

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:48.120
What's weak against this type of magic or this element?

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:52.440
Yeah, so there's the four swords that you get in the game, each with a different element.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:58.480
And as Joe mentioned, it also becomes a little, not like a puzzle per se,

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:03.660
but, like, a way to block off or require you to use the correct magic

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.860
and switch your weapons accordingly as well.

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:12.480
Did you find that to be a tedious thing that you have to kind of go into the menus

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:14.200
and switch out between swords?

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:17.120
Or was that strategic?

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:19.280
I think they could have done it better.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:21.980
I like the idea of it.

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:23.660
I think it's a great game mechanic.

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:28.840
I think literally the actual literal mechanics of how you switch can get tedious.

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:33.940
Because a lot of times you'll have two or three enemy types in one area right in front of you,

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:36.960
and they take damage from different things.

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:41.440
You know, they're immune to one thing while the other enemy takes damage from that same thing.

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:45.780
So to have to press select and cycle through your menu, change your sword,

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:50.360
and change your, like, magical item every time you want to attack a different enemy

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:52.120
is a little annoying.

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:56.320
And I was eventually watching some videos about it,

00:18:56.340 --> 00:19:02.900
and one video suggested, like, you have your start menu and you have your select menu.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.700
Your start menu gives you, like, some stats and, like, your pause and your save.

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:09.360
And your select menu, you can scroll through all your power-ups that you want to change.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:12.640
Like, both of them you could scroll from, like, page one, page two, page three.

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:16.680
So really all of those things should be on the start menu if you want to do that.

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:21.040
And then select should just be, like, a quick change between, like, your three swords or your four swords.

00:19:21.340 --> 00:19:23.620
That would make this game, I think, a lot smoother.

00:19:24.020 --> 00:19:25.020
That would help.

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:29.540
I think that even with that, this really wouldn't have landed well.

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:31.860
I agree that it's kind of tedious.

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:39.240
I think that it's a cool idea, but this is not the console to do it on.

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:40.960
Because you only have so many buttons.

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:47.400
And when I think of a battle system or a combat system like this,

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:50.960
as weird as it sounds, I think of Doom Eternal.

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:55.240
You have so many different ways to kill enemies.

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:58.980
Like, whether it's your guns, which gun you're using.

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:01.820
You've got, like, a flamethrower backpack thing.

00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:03.960
You've got a chainsaw.

00:20:04.180 --> 00:20:07.700
And all those things have different effects on different enemies.

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:11.640
And have different effects on the kind of resources that you can gather.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:17.120
But you have enough buttons on the controller or on your keyboard to do all that.

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:21.920
The fact that you have to go into a menu of any kind or scroll through,

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:29.340
which may also, like, hamper the, just how, like, again, like,

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:35.160
I was praising how there's very little friction from doing what you want to do,

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:38.920
or from going from what you want to do to doing what you want to do in this game.

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:40.720
That adds friction.

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:46.380
And if you have to go into a menu, I just don't think that that's really a valid concept.

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:51.060
I mean, the reason that I don't, like, fully dislike it in theory

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:56.820
is because I like a JRPG where I have to change equipment.

00:20:57.240 --> 00:21:01.220
Or, you know, and, like, this kind of takes that element into this more action RPG.

00:21:01.820 --> 00:21:05.400
And, like, if they could have pulled it off right, it would be, it wouldn't have to be,

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:08.280
I'm not imagining it being, like, I've never played Doom Eternal,

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:12.380
but, like, anything like that where it's, like, I'm literally in mid-action,

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:14.260
like, use this attack, then that attack, then this attack.

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:16.980
It's more like, I see this enemy, I'll pull out this sword.

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:18.520
I see that enemy, I'll pull out that sword.

00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:20.320
And if they could have let me do that a little faster,

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:23.120
I think that would give it some depth that I would like more.

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:25.180
And, like, I didn't hate it here.

00:21:26.120 --> 00:21:28.100
Again, like, I didn't hate having to change.

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:29.780
I hated how long it took me to change.

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:43.220
I thought it was interesting that the very first spell you learn in the game is a recover spell.

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:47.840
The ability to just replenish HP whenever, wherever.

00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:53.140
And I know, like, you know, in a JRPG game where you have a party,

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:57.300
usually there's, like, a white mage-style character who can restore people's health or whatever.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:04.200
But in a solo ARPG-style game, the ability to just kind of, as long as you have MP,

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:09.380
heal whenever, wherever, was also, like, refreshingly different.

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:16.520
And a lot of the spells that you learn, too, are all defensive-based, you know?

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:22.600
There's the barrier one to block projectiles, the recover one to heal yourself from poison,

00:22:24.580 --> 00:22:28.160
eventually change forms, teleport out of a place.

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:34.080
I just feel like, you know, it's nice to, obviously, the force power things have, like,

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:36.340
your moves based on the element of the swords.

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:40.920
But it's nice that you don't have to, like, also learn the fireball, right?

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:43.980
Or, like, just the base-level attacks of those things.

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:48.460
And then, like, as you level up, it's like you went from Fyra to Firaga, right?

00:22:48.460 --> 00:22:55.600
It's like, instead, you're just actually getting, like, things that change or improve your character.

00:22:55.860 --> 00:23:00.940
Yeah, I was always more interested in utility-type spells.

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:05.200
That isn't just, like, a different way to do damage.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:09.740
Because, you know, it's just more creative, I think.

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:13.520
And I think that this sort of handled that well.

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:17.080
Like, you were saying, like, oh, fire, Fyra.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:23.980
Going from that to that isn't as interesting as finding a new way to just interact with the world.

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:27.740
Mm-hmm. Yeah. And, I mean, that came with spells and with items.

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:28.860
Like, you get the ability to jump.

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:29.900
I think that was an item, right?

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:31.940
But, like, the same thing.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:33.620
Like, there's spells that let you do...

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:36.860
I mean, there's a spell that lets you communicate with the elders.

00:23:37.420 --> 00:23:39.060
Mm-hmm. Which is very strange.

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:42.260
And, like, I'm not sure how I feel about how I was executed.

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:44.380
But an interesting thing.

00:23:44.560 --> 00:23:45.820
You know, because it's like...

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:46.720
It's a fortune-tree spell.

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:48.380
You use a spell and you just get, like...

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:50.480
You get a piece of information from one of the elders.

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:52.440
But then it, like, takes up a lot of your MP.

00:23:52.740 --> 00:23:56.660
And, I don't know. I guess it's a cool way to have a little guidance, you know, pre-internet.

00:23:57.080 --> 00:23:59.220
Yeah, they just feel different, you know?

00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:03.340
And, Sean, you were talking about how you like using the utility stuff.

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:04.520
I agree now.

00:24:04.520 --> 00:24:09.440
Like, I do like trying to play different than I would have as a kid.

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:11.920
But as a kid, I totally was...

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:13.120
Like, even in Pokemon, right?

00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:15.380
It's like, don't give me Tail Whip.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:16.760
Like, I never want Tail Whip.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:20.120
I'm only ever going to do offensive damage on these.

00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:23.680
And you don't realize until later on when you start, you know, thinking about, like,

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:32.700
Oh, well, it turns out, actually, if I would have just, you know, used a spell to raise my attack before attacking,

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:34.580
I would have taken less damage, right?

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:36.320
It's like a thing you have to overcome.

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:38.200
It's a game theory thing.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:51.260
But the game here mostly offers you just ways to kind of stay in the battle as opposed to, you know, fight harder or do more damage.

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:59.140
Again, I guess maybe as a result of the fact that sometimes you just can't damage things if you're not at the level that you need to be.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:03.920
But that's only really a problem, too, like, at the beginning of the game.

00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:08.800
Like, eventually you get used to the grind as you get used to any kind of grind in a game, right?

00:25:08.960 --> 00:25:14.320
Like, any Final Fantasy game that requires you to grind your party.

00:25:14.540 --> 00:25:18.380
It's like you usually die to a surprise boss early on.

00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:23.180
And you're like, okay, going forward, I'm just going to be overleveled.

00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:24.380
Am I speaking out of turn?

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:25.400
Is that just a me thing?

00:25:25.400 --> 00:25:28.760
No, I think that's definitely a way.

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:32.040
I think it's a coping mechanism.

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:42.580
I've never been a fan of grinding, whether it's been in games I love or in games that I otherwise wouldn't be that into.

00:25:43.880 --> 00:25:53.000
But I understand that it's going to exist and I will miss out if I use that as a reason not to play a game.

00:25:53.120 --> 00:25:54.460
So I'll do it.

00:25:54.460 --> 00:26:05.020
And I think, like, the reason that I brought it up as a specific point in this game isn't really like, oh, I got, like, curb stomped in this certain point.

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:14.520
Or, like, oh, I got annoyed that I went to the mountain and everything was giving me, like, that tinky noise, which means it wasn't doing any damage.

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:16.760
It was the sword fight.

00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:24.520
Again, like, that, like, you're sort of forced into doing before you can move forward at, like, this training house.

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:31.020
And you have to just, like, hammer on the A button to, like, back this guy into a corner.

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:36.920
And I realized that, like, at the level I was at, I was just, like, I just, like, you're never dying.

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:38.120
It's just, like, you keep losing.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:43.840
But then I leveled up again and I could last a little bit longer, but it still wasn't working.

00:26:43.980 --> 00:26:45.600
And then I had to level up again.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:51.420
And I'm like, Jesus, I can only fight the same mobs outside for so long without getting bored.

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:54.000
So this is, like, an early section.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:55.100
Is that level-based?

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:56.940
I didn't realize that had to do with your level.

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:01.480
I feel like it had to based on how I experienced it.

00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:04.000
How many times did you try before you started grinding?

00:27:04.760 --> 00:27:06.060
A few times.

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:08.540
And I just kept getting pushed into the back of the wall.

00:27:08.840 --> 00:27:14.320
Because I read something about, like, literally, like, the first three times you can't win or something like that.

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:17.060
No way. And then it lets you, like, you have more of a chance.

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:19.780
That's actually bullshit.

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:21.960
Yeah, that's stupid. I kind of hate this game now.

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:26.440
Because it made me think that I had to go out and grind.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:29.900
So I waited until I got to the next level, then went back.

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:32.520
So maybe I was overleveled for a couple minutes.

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:36.740
The good news is you almost certainly would have had to have grinded very shortly after that anyways.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:38.820
So you got it out of the way.

00:27:39.020 --> 00:27:44.520
But as far as the grind goes, I mean, there's no denying that this game requires a lot of grinding.

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:49.700
But I wondered if it requires a lot of grinding for us today.

00:27:50.680 --> 00:27:54.060
Playing with, like, a little bit of guides and a little bit, you know, or I don't know about you guys.

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:59.280
But, you know, I did, like I said, I started eventually playing with a guide just to get further in a shorter amount of time.

00:28:00.100 --> 00:28:12.800
And I did notice that before I started with the guide, I never really felt like I needed the grind because my exploration, as long as I just constantly fought things that were on the screen as I was trying to figure out where to go, I kept a pretty good level.

00:28:12.800 --> 00:28:17.640
And I can't say that it would have stayed that way had I continued through the whole game like that.

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:27.940
But I did notice that, like, shortly after I started using a guide and progressing really quickly, I obviously was not leveling up at a rate that matched how far I was progressing to the game.

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:32.580
But then I needed to, like, look more often and be like, okay, I'm underleveled.

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:34.040
No, I agree.

00:28:34.040 --> 00:28:51.460
I only sparingly used the guide, which is also, which also explains where I ended the game, which is still in that mountain just searching around caves, which I believe is pretty early in the game.

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:55.100
But I would, I agree.

00:28:55.100 --> 00:29:04.940
The amount of time that I would have had to looked around to find that stupid statue probably would have put me in a pretty good spot.

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:14.360
And I know, like, there is a quest giver that basically says, like, hey, I found, I lost a statue, it's over here.

00:29:14.740 --> 00:29:19.160
And I could tell you that I would eventually have found it.

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:29.420
But it's vague enough that with the amount of game that I was trying to get through, it just wasn't going to be acceptable to search for that long.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:38.300
Yeah. But something like that definitely would have been a roadblock where I would have naturally grinded or just naturally gained levels, I guess.

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:43.080
Yeah. And also, I actually forgot about that statue because I just totally lucked out and found it.

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:50.800
But thinking back, if I hadn't been lucky and just, like, I wasn't even trying to find it, I just walked on the spot and it said, like, oh, you found a statue.

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:52.960
That part is a little bit bullshit, too.

00:29:53.200 --> 00:29:55.840
Because there's no, like, statue on, there's nothing on screen.

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:57.360
There's no straighter. Yeah, just in the rooms.

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:00.300
There's a walk onto this square and the statue will be there.

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:07.300
So, yeah, I lucked out, but I do, I can see an alternate universe where I would be very upset about how annoying that was.

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:17.160
And I think a lot of games back then, you know, wanted you to explore and poke everything anyway, right?

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:20.920
To extend the longevity of the game.

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:34.020
But, Sean, you mentioned earlier how, like, a dragon quest, for instance, Dragon Warrior, would have, you know, menued everything out, going downstairs, talking to people, etc.

00:30:34.020 --> 00:30:39.440
But in Crystals, you have a funny way of talking to people when you explore the towns and everything.

00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:41.600
And that's just, you run into them.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:46.120
Yeah, it's also annoying, but at least, I'd say it's the lesser of two evils here.

00:30:46.360 --> 00:30:54.980
Yes, I would agree that it's the lesser of two evils, but I did wind up having a lot of duplicate conversations with people that I didn't mean to talk to.

00:30:55.420 --> 00:30:57.080
If that's what you were saying by annoying.

00:30:57.320 --> 00:31:03.180
Oh, yeah. Yeah, and you can get, like, you can sort of get cornered.

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:05.320
Oh, wait, no. No, you can't.

00:31:05.420 --> 00:31:06.600
I'm thinking of something else.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:12.420
Yeah, it's okay. But it's like, you know, running into people to talk is kind of, it's funny, right?

00:31:12.460 --> 00:31:13.560
That's like an expression, right?

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:14.980
Like, oh, look who I ran into.

00:31:15.580 --> 00:31:16.680
Yeah, that's why they did that.

00:31:16.920 --> 00:31:18.020
That's why they did it, right?

00:31:18.020 --> 00:31:21.960
But this is going to lead into my next question about just the towns in general.

00:31:22.540 --> 00:31:27.720
Were they just kind of lore dumps or were they interesting places?

00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:36.680
Was it always just, you know, here's the inn, here's the old sage, here's, like, the people walking around the town, and here's the shop?

00:31:36.680 --> 00:31:40.820
Or were there, like, you know, I remember at 1.2, there's, like, a bar.

00:31:41.620 --> 00:31:48.340
Were the towns interesting and unique enough that the world continued to feel alive as you moved on through the game, I guess is my question.

00:31:49.220 --> 00:31:51.500
Um, you know, yes and no.

00:31:51.860 --> 00:31:53.960
Like, some of the towns definitely were samey.

00:31:54.100 --> 00:32:00.820
You know, you get different layouts. You get different, yeah, information from villagers or whatever or quests from them.

00:32:00.980 --> 00:32:02.240
But, yeah, there's a lot of saminess.

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:07.920
But there's some that, like, there's one town a little ways in where there's, like, a castle in the town.

00:32:07.920 --> 00:32:11.900
And there's stuff in there to sort of explore and people to talk to in there.

00:32:11.980 --> 00:32:14.900
Like, the queen or the fortune teller or whoever she is.

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:18.700
So there's some character to those.

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:20.920
But there are also plenty of cookie cutter towns.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:27.320
I'd say, like, the highlight of what I was able to play through would have been the dwarf town in the swamp.

00:32:27.560 --> 00:32:31.500
Where, you know, the first time you happen upon it, they won't even talk to you.

00:32:32.760 --> 00:32:38.960
And then you will learn of, like, oh, there's a kid that's trapped out there.

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:41.420
And you go and you rescue the kid.

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:47.520
And it's also, like, a really fun little bit of, uh, just a little accent.

00:32:47.780 --> 00:32:51.240
Where once you rescue the kid, he doesn't run as fast as you.

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:55.400
So you kind of have to keep, like, doubling back to make sure he's with you.

00:32:55.500 --> 00:33:01.780
Which is just, like, a little bit of, uh, of detail that I just wasn't expecting out of an NES game.

00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:04.500
Uh, anyway. So then you bring him back.

00:33:04.600 --> 00:33:06.180
And then, like, oh, thank god.

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:09.980
Like, uh, maybe we can trust you.

00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:17.000
Um, by the way, there's, like, this, this, uh, this giant enemy that has been harassing the town.

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:18.240
Could you take care of that?

00:33:18.320 --> 00:33:19.720
And then you go and you take care of it.

00:33:19.880 --> 00:33:22.800
And now they're even, like, then they'll give you another gift.

00:33:22.800 --> 00:33:25.900
And, like, that town, you know, it's all very simple.

00:33:25.900 --> 00:33:33.140
And when we think about progression or just, like, the world changing around you in modern games.

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:39.320
Uh, but it felt very reactive, uh, for the era that we're in right now.

00:33:39.500 --> 00:33:41.440
So I thought that was pretty cool.

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:46.200
Going from, like, they will always give you the cold shoulder to you're the hero of the village.

00:33:46.900 --> 00:33:48.240
Yeah, I really like that, too.

00:33:48.240 --> 00:33:54.060
Like, you, you are, you are living within this world and your, your actions are affecting things.

00:33:54.220 --> 00:33:57.520
